        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 853,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 721,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the modified Bessel function of the first kind of order 0, I0, to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt shapes the Kaiser window used in filter design and is the normalising constant of the von Mises distribution (a bell curve wrapped around a circle, for angles and directions).\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at 0, symmetric, curving up on both sides and then growing roughly like e to the |x| (divided by the square root of 2 pi |x|): I0(2) = 2.28, I0(4) = 11.3. It never oscillates - it is the Bessel equation with a sign flipped.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nOverflows to infinity past about |x| = 90 in 32-bit floats;\nt.special.i0e avoids that.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I0(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4, drawn from 0 (bottom) to 12 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i0e is the same divided by e to the |x|, which stays in range. t.special.i1 is order 1. t.special.bessel_j0 is the oscillating cousin. t.window.kaiser builds the window this function shapes.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 705,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the modified Bessel function of the first kind of order 0, I0, to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt shapes the Kaiser window used in filter design and is the normalising constant of the von Mises distribution (a bell curve wrapped around a circle, for angles and directions).\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at 0, symmetric, curving up on both sides and then growing roughly like e to the |x| (divided by the square root of 2 pi |x|): I0(2) = 2.28, I0(4) = 11.3. It never oscillates - it is the Bessel equation with a sign flipped.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nOverflows to infinity past about |x| = 90 in 32-bit floats;\nt.special.i0e avoids that.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-4 to 4 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I0(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -4 to 4, drawn from 0 (bottom) to 12 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i0e is the same divided by e to the |x|, which stays in range. t.special.i1 is order 1. t.special.bessel_j0 is the oscillating cousin. t.window.kaiser builds the window this function shapes."
        },
